\chapter{Testing}\label{Testing}

The tests live in the same files as the code they verify, right after it,
and [[make test]] tangles them into [[tests/test_*.py]] and runs [[pytest]].
This chapter covers what the individual tests share: how a test gets a LADOK
session, and which tests may talk to LADOK at all.

\section{Three kinds of tests}

The tests fall into three groups, by what they need from LADOK.
\begin{description}
\item[Offline tests] exercise our own code: parsing, the command-line
  interface, the reporting logic, session sharing.
  They use real objects where they can and small local stand-ins where a
  LADOK response is needed.
  They need no network and no credentials, and they are most of the
  tests.
\item[Live read tests] send requests to LADOK's test environment and check
  the responses.
  Most of them are in the chapters on the API calls.
\item[Live write tests] create, update, finalize and remove results in the
  test environment.
  They are marked [[mutates]].
\end{description}

LADOK's test environment is not a sandbox filled with made-up data.
It is a copy of production, synced again every week, so the students in it
are real people and a write changes a real record until the next sync.
Logging in also takes multi-factor authentication, which needs a person at
hand.
For both reasons the live tests are opt-in:
\begin{description}
\item[[[make test]] ] runs only the offline tests and skips the rest, with
  the reason.
\item[[[make test PYTESTFLAGS=--live]] ] adds the live read tests.
\item[[[make test PYTESTFLAGS="--live --live-writes"]] ] adds the write
  tests as well.
\end{description}

Earlier, the test modules created their session at import time.
That had two bad effects.
Without credentials, the import failed, so [[pytest]] could not collect the
module, and its offline tests never ran either.
With credentials, a plain [[make test]] was a live run: it logged in, could
prompt for MFA and wrote results, and [[make publish]] runs [[make test]]
before every release.
Taking the session from a fixture fixes both problems.
A fixture runs only when a test asks for it, so it can skip that test
instead of failing the import, and an offline run never creates a session
at all.

\section{The shared test configuration}

[[pytest]] reads command-line options and shared fixtures from a file
[[conftest.py]] in the test directory, which we tangle from here.
<<[[conftest.py]]>>=
"""Shared pytest configuration for ladok3's tests.

Tangled from src/ladok3/testing.nw; edit that file, not this one.
"""
import ladok3
import pytest

<<command-line options>>
<<test markers>>
<<fixtures>>
<<collection rules>>
@

\subsection{Opting in to live runs}

The two options are separate because they carry different risks.
A read test only looks at the data, while a write test changes it.
<<command-line options>>=
def pytest_addoption(parser):
  group = parser.getgroup("ladok3")
  group.addoption(
    "--live", action="store_true",
    help="also run the tests against LADOK's test environment "
         "(attended: may prompt for MFA)")
  group.addoption(
    "--live-writes", action="store_true",
    help="with --live, also run the tests that change data in LADOK's "
         "test environment")
@

We register the [[mutates]] marker so that [[pytest]] knows it and a
misspelling is reported instead of silently matching nothing.
<<test markers>>=
def pytest_configure(config):
  config.addinivalue_line(
    "markers",
    "mutates: the test changes data in LADOK; "
    "runs only with --live --live-writes")
@

A [[mutates]] test is skipped unless we asked for writes.
Without [[--live]] it is skipped anyway, by the [[ladok]] fixture below, but
with [[--live]] alone, this rule is what keeps it from running.
<<collection rules>>=
def pytest_collection_modifyitems(config, items):
  if config.getoption("--live") and config.getoption("--live-writes"):
    return
  skip_writes = pytest.mark.skip(
    reason="changes data in LADOK; run with --live --live-writes")
  for item in items:
    if "mutates" in item.keywords:
      item.add_marker(skip_writes)
@

\subsection{Sessions for the tests}

Every live test depends on the [[live]] fixture, directly or through one of
the session fixtures, and that fixture is what skips the test in an offline
run.
<<fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def live(request):
  """Skip the requesting test unless pytest runs with `--live`."""
  if not request.config.getoption("--live"):
    pytest.skip("needs LADOK's test environment; run with --live")
@

The live tests share one session, [[ladok3.test_session]].
It reuses the cached login from [[ladok login]] (\cref{CachedSession}), so a
live run usually needs no new MFA prompt.
When there are no stored credentials, [[ladok3.test_session]] raises
[[RuntimeError]], and we skip rather than fail: the problem is the machine's
set-up, not the code.
Any other error is left to fail the test, because it may well be a bug in
the login code.
<<fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def ladok(live):
  """The shared session for LADOK's test environment."""
  try:
    return ladok3.test_session
  except RuntimeError as err:
    pytest.skip(f"cannot log in to LADOK: {err}")
@

Some tests check validation that happens before any request is sent, for
instance that [[fetch_linked_uri_JSON]] refuses a link to another host.
They need a [[LadokSession]] object, but not a logged-in one.
Creating the object makes no request, since the login happens on the first
request, so these tests run offline.
If one of them sent a request by mistake, it would fail, because that
request would trigger a login without credentials.
<<fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def offline_ladok():
  """A session for LADOK's test environment that never logs in."""
  return ladok3.LadokSession("KTH Royal Institute of Technology",
                             test_environment=True)
@
